## Building an Environmental Economy...

America has the technology and resources to build a serious new environmental economy, but yet, we remain at odds with our own legal system. Developing an opperational environmental economy requires more than inventing cleaner technologies, it also requires institutions that can approve, finance, and construct projects while maintaining public trust. The readings by Potter, Doomberg, Howard, and Wang suggest that the challenge is not simply environmental protection versus economic growth, but how to design a system that can achieve both.

When discussing the regulatory and legal challenges being barriers to development, Doomberg and Potter argue two sides of the same coin. Doomberg argues that when it comes to the barriers of development, it is the legal system itself that is flawed; creating a system in which litigation can become a business. Potter agrees that the problem lays within the legal system, but instead focuses on the incentives and sources of delay within environmental review and permitting
